WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.520
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology Professor.&nbsp;
Today, I want to talk to you about the life cycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:24.270
of a pretty terrible parasite called hookworm.&nbsp;
Now, hookworm is a parasite, specifically a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:30.690
kind of helminth, which means a worm, which we can&nbsp;
tell from its name. So hookworm is a parasite that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:42.540
infects upwards of 800 million people per year&nbsp;
globally, so certainly a serious parasite for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:48.960
people's experiences. The worm is particularly&nbsp;
noteworthy because it actually penetrates right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:01:04.080
through unbroken skin of the host. So a lot of&nbsp;
times, various pathogens will actually need a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.410
cut in the skin to be able to enter through the&nbsp;
skin. Hookworm doesn't need that. It can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:19.620
burrow right through healthy unbroken skin. And&nbsp;
ultimately it lives in the small intestine where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:33.180
it attaches to the intestinal walls, sometimes for&nbsp;
years, and sucks the host's blood. Now this leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:42.810
to some pretty severe side effects resulting&nbsp;
from anemia, that is the blood is sucked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:50.730
blood is where a lot of our body's iron is&nbsp;
stored, and so hosts get anemia, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:57.270
an iron deficiency and the anemia then goes on&nbsp;
to cause its own problems, including delayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:07.050
growth and delayed mental development or mental&nbsp;
impairment. Now, we said hookworm is a parasite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:17.040
parasite is actually a form of symbiosis, which is&nbsp;
where two organisms live in really close contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:26.610
with each other. In particular, parasitism is what&nbsp;
we call a plus/minus form, that means that of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:33.000
two organisms involved in that symbiotic event,&nbsp;
so the two organisms here being the hookworm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:42.030
and its host, one of those organisms benefits&nbsp;
from the symbiosis, the benefitter here would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:50.010
be the hookworm, and then the other organism is&nbsp;
harmed and of course in the case of all events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:56.100
parasitism, the one being harmed is the host.&nbsp;
If you are interested in symbiosis and about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.800
learning more about parasitism and other forms of&nbsp;
symbiosis, including mutualism and commensalism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:08.580
please see my video on the different types&nbsp;
of symbiosis. But to continue here, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:20.220
now talk about the lifecycle of hookworm. Now a&nbsp;
hookworm infection begins with an egg. Now eggs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:32.640
hookworm eggs are shed in human feces, so an&nbsp;
infected host has hookworms inside them, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:41.940
feces will contain huge, huge numbers of hookworm&nbsp;
eggs. And this is actually the diagnostic stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:51.330
meaning that you can take a fecal sample from a&nbsp;
person and actually see under a microscope all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:58.230
the different eggs in the feces, and so that's how&nbsp;
a hookworm infection is typically diagnosed. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:12.390
these eggs, if they make it into soil, they&nbsp;
can actually hatch in the warm soil into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:22.170
next developmental stage which is larvae, and they&nbsp;
need warm soil to do this and this is why hookworm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:28.260
is typically thought of as a tropical disease&nbsp;
because it's those tropical temperatures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:36.960
allow the favorable conditions necessary for&nbsp;
the eggs to hatch into larvae. Now this early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:45.240
larval form has a particular name, it's called&nbsp;
the rhabditiform stage of the larvae. Now, over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:54.000
the course of a couple of weeks, the rhabditiform&nbsp;
form larvae will mature into a more mature form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:01.530
the larval stage here is called filariform, and&nbsp;
these larvae can actually survive approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:10.770
three to four weeks in the soil. And that is&nbsp;
when conditions are particularly favorable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:19.650
so when temperature and moisture conditions are&nbsp;
right. Now, it is these filariform larvae that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:28.170
can actually burrow through the host skin. So here&nbsp;
we have a picture, a drawing, of a human foot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:34.830
these larvae can actually burrow right through&nbsp;
the skin. Now the larvae are going to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:42.720
burrow through whatever skin is most in contact&nbsp;
with the soil, which of course is where they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:53.700
living, and so typically this is done when a human&nbsp;
goes barefoot and has their bare foot in contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:06:03.420
with the soil. Now once this larvae enters the&nbsp;
human body, it goes through a really circuitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:10.320
route. First, it enters the bloodstream, from the&nbsp;
bloodstream it moves to the heart, from the heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:21.420
it can travel through the body to the lungs,&nbsp;
and then the larvae actually gets coughed up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:27.870
so it gets coughed up from the lungs, up&nbsp;
to the pharynx, and then it gets swallowed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:36.240
and goes to the stomach. And the larvae that&nbsp;
survives the stomach acid conditions make it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.780
the small intestines, and it is here in the small&nbsp;
intestine where the larvae mature into adults.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:56.970
So the larvae mature into adult worms. These adult&nbsp;
worms are the ones that then attach to the lining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:04.050
of the small intestine and suck blood. They can&nbsp;
stay there for years and during the time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:12.090
they are alive within the body, they're constantly&nbsp;
laying eggs, huge huge numbers of eggs that then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:22.260
get excreted from the human body in the feces and&nbsp;
then the cycle can continue. Now, luckily hookworm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:28.980
has been all but eradicated in most developed&nbsp;
nations and this is for a few different reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:37.440
First, we have anti-helminthic drugs, so these are&nbsp;
drugs that fight worm infections. We can also take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:45.990
iron pills to counteract the anemia that comes&nbsp;
from the blood loss. And then we can also break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:55.050
this this chain of transmission by doing sort&nbsp;
of two main things: encouraging people to wear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.050 --> 00:08:01.830
shoes and making sure that everybody has shoes,&nbsp;
this is of course a bigger problem in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:07.890
developing nations, and also we can break this&nbsp;
chain of transmission in another place as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:12.930
and that's right here: making sure that the&nbsp;
human feces that contains the eggs does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:20.280
actually get into soil. And of course this can&nbsp;
be done when countries have proper sanitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.770
and public health infrastructure, and that's why&nbsp;
we don't really hear about hookworm in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:33.450
States anymore but certainly it is a bigger&nbsp;
problem in many other parts of the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:39.390
you can see that from the number who are infected.&nbsp;
Now, if you are interested in learning about other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:45.960
types of parasite life cycles, please see my other&nbsp;
video which is on the life cycle of the parasite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:53.570
that causes the the disease schistosomiasis,&nbsp;
and that's another kind of worm parasite so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:57.103
check that one out if you're interested.&nbsp;
Thanks for watching Biology Professor! :)

